  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana 9.5.5.\n\n**Data exploration.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` view (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) holds web access logs. The main fields are `clientip`, `bytes`, `response` and `url` (each with a `.keyword` subfield for aggregation), plus `geo.*`, `agent`, `machine.os` and `extension`. I could not query the data directly: Elasticsearch is not reachable on localhost:9200, and Kibana's query proxies returned 400. So the dashboard panels and the rule's 5xx filter rest on the field mappings, not on checked values.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-092a2010 Web logs overview`** (ID `eff-092a2010-web-logs-overview-5fa8746a8038`, default 7-day range) has six panels:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Average response size (average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time by response code (bar, date histogram split by `response.keyword`)\n- Response code distribution (pie by `response.keyword`)\n- Top requested URLs (table of count and bytes by `url.keyword`)\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-092a2010 5xx spike`** (ID `a9561d6c-fb29-4d45-a330-8ba810b1e611`):\n- Type `.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`, runs every 1m.\n- Query: `response.keyword` wildcard `5*` over the last 5 minutes, fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- I enabled it after creating it, because a disabled rule would never check anything. Disable it with `disable_alert_rule` if you want it off.\n- It has no action connector, so it records alerts in the Stack Alerts view but sends no notifications.",
